    "q-184122": {
      "dom_id": "step-q184122",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=6q-3,$ where $q\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument showing that $n$ is odd. \n\nThe number $n$ is odd since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6q-3 \\\\  & =2({{field-1}})+1 \\\\  & ={{field-2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=$ {{field-3}}, which is an integer.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a+1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is odd since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6q-3 \\\\  & =(6q-4)+1 \\\\  & =2({{3q-2}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=3q-2,$ which is an integer.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
